<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Users + Profiles' cross-section surface. Carved ahead of the section move; a later change
    added the three entities and the write interface Base still names.

    A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Users was never an option here. UserInfo is the
    canonical "everything-about-a-person" read model and IUserServiceRead is injected by
    HumansControllerBase, so between them they are named by more than twenty section
    projects, by Humans.UI, and — decisively — by Humans.Application and
    Humans.Infrastructure, which the section itself references. A folder would cycle at
    the assembly level in both directions (design §15 step 5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    It holds exactly what lives outside the section: the UserInfo read model and the eight
    projections it nests, the cross-section read interface and its result shapes, the
    service interfaces something outside the section injects, and — the three additions
    PR B forced, each because a *Base* consumer names it and Base cannot reference a
    section:

      User / UserEmail / EventParticipation.  48
      files across Shell, CityPlanning, Development and twenty test projects
      spell UserManager<User> / SignInManager<User> / AddIdentity<User, …>. ASP.NET
      Identity's generic surface cannot be hidden, so User is public here rather than
      internal in the section, and its two nav collections drag UserEmail and
      EventParticipation with it (CS0053 otherwise). The other six entities — Profile,
      ContactField, ProfileLanguage, VolunteerHistoryEntry, CommunicationPreference,
      AccountMergeRequest — are internal in Humans.Users/Domain/ as the Camps precedent
      wants.

      The three Users/Profiles enums (UserState, ContactFieldType,
      ContactFieldVisibility), which PR A could not take because Humans.Domain's entities
      declared columns of those types. The entities are here now, so the cycle is gone.

      IUserService and its command/result records.  Peter's ruling was "public on
      Humans.Users, not in the leaf", with the eleven write-calling section projects
      taking a project reference on the section. That is unreachable alongside the §I
      ruling that AccountDeletionService, ExternalLoginService and
      UserParticipationBackfillService stay in Humans.Application as orchestrators: those
      three plus HumanLifecycleService and three Humans.Infrastructure jobs inject
      IUserService from *Base*, and Humans.Users references both Base projects. The
      approved surface is unchanged — IUserService is public either way — only the
      assembly moved, which is design §15 step 5b's own rule ("a consumer in Base forces
      Humans.<Section>.Contracts"). It also means no consumer needed a new
      project reference at all.

    Still NOT here, and staying internal to the section: IAccountMergeService,
    IDuplicateAccountService, IUnsubscribeService, IUnsubscribeTokenProvider,
    IEmailProblemsService, IProfilePictureService and
    the six repository interfaces.

    UserInfo.Create stays here: it takes the eight EF entities, six of which are now
    internal to the section, so the section owns an internal factory
    (Humans.Users.Services.UserInfoFactory) and the record keeps a primitive-only
    Create for Base and the test projects.

    ONE project reference, AND IT MUST NEVER GROW. Base (Humans.Interfaces, becoming
    Humans.Base at 4b-iv) references THIS project, because HumansControllerBase injects
    IUserServiceRead and Humans.UI names the User entity. So anything reachable from here is
    reachable from Base, and a reference added here to any project that has references of its
    own is a cycle the moment 4b-iii moves HumansControllerBase/ApiControllerBase into Base.
    That cycle is not live today — Humans.Interfaces still has zero project references of its
    own — so a green build proves nothing about this rule. The invariant is textual, not a
    build outcome.

    THE RULE: this project may reference only a leaf that itself has zero project references.
    Such a chain terminates, so it cannot cycle no matter what Base does. The single surviving
    reference, Humans.Onboarding.Contracts, satisfies it: that leaf has zero project references
    and its csproj now says in terms that it must stay that way. Base -> Users.Contracts ->
    Onboarding.Contracts -> nothing.

    Why OnboardingResult was not moved to close it out. It is the onboarding funnel's own
    result vocabulary, returned by IOnboardingIntake.RejectSignupAsync,
    IAccountDeletionService's two methods, IHumanLifecycleService's three and
    IUserService.ApplyProfileOnboardingMutationAsync. Pulling it here would force
    Humans.Onboarding.Contracts to reference this project to keep its own signature — trading
    one acyclic edge for a worse one and taking the zero-reference property off the leaf that
    is load-bearing for it. Duplicating it (Humans.Auth.Contracts.RoleAssignmentResult's
    shape) would put a third copy of a two-property record in the graph and grow this
    project's public surface, which needs Peter. Neither is worth it: the edge is provably
    harmless and the flat "zero references" phrasing of the G5 end-state design is the thing
    that is imprecise, not this csproj. State the rule above, not the count.

    Also gone: Humans.Interfaces, which owned the IApplicationService / IOrchestrator /
    IInvalidator marker interfaces and the [Grandfathered] / [ExpiresOn] attributes — none of
    this project's interfaces carry them now, and analyzers key on the implementing classes
    instead (MarkdownContentAttribute is a deliberate internal twin here, not a move, because
    eight section projects apply the original). And Humans.Domain, whose last enums —
    ConsentCheckStatus, GoogleEmailStatus, MembershipTier, MessageCategory — moved here; no
    member was renamed, so nothing persisted moved.

    THREE leaves gain an edge to this project as a consequence, all acyclic under the same
    rule — this project is itself terminal-but-one and none of them is reachable from it:
    Humans.Governance.Contracts (MembershipTier), Humans.Teams.Contracts (GoogleEmailStatus)
    and Humans.Email.Contracts (MembershipTier + MessageCategory). The phase-3 recon
    predicted only the first two; the third was found by the compiler. Same shape, same
    reasoning — an enum's consumers follow it to its owner.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Humans.Onboarding.Contracts\Humans.Onboarding.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- User : IdentityUser<Guid> — the ASP.NET Identity principal type. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
